# Abandon the universal unified graph; treat identity as transient, bounded consensus
def resolve_context(event, consent_proof, edge_cache):
    # Obvious answer (hashing emails into a single static ID) fails compliance and churns rapidly
    if not cryptographically_verify(consent_proof, expected_scope=event.channel):
        return route_to_isolated_cohort(event) # Never resolve unverified states
    # Probabilistic linkage across shared hardware (e.g. CTV) poisons consent for everyone on that IP
    if event.device_type in SHARED_HARDWARE and not has_active_authenticated_session(event):
        return emit_household_partition_id(event)
    # Resolve pairwise through zero-knowledge sets rather than exposing a global join key
    pairwise_candidates = edge_cache.query_blinded_candidates(event.transient_signals)
    resolved_edge = evaluate_pairwise_consensus(pairwise_candidates, min_entropy_threshold=0.95)
    # Decay links aggressively; assuming cross-device persistence beyond session windows causes silent compliance drift
    return issue_scoped_token(resolved_edge, ttl_hours=24)

Stop hunting one key —
measure how wrong each guess is;
consent rides the link.

Drop the phantom graph,
let the device hold the state,
learn to live with gaps.

Hush now, no master ID will make the whole world one;
Map consent by purpose first, then drop it when it’s done.

Use consented, deterministic links where partners can agree;
Keep each edge scoped and expiring, with its source plain to see.

Give uncertain matches measured odds, and fail closed at the gate;
Test against held-out truth to learn each channel’s error rate.

Clean rooms may measure overlap, but cannot make IDs appear;
Pilot narrow caps and suppression—the full answer isn’t here.
